Do not write process memory into minidumps.

Crashpad captures every thread's stack plus the "extra memory" the snapshot
classes ask for. On a wallet those regions can hold secrets (mnemonics, PINs,
passwords, private keys), and the minidump is uploaded to Sentry, so we strip
them at the source instead of relying on server-side scrubbing. Every source
has to go or the dump still carries memory:

  ThreadSnapshot::Stack()            each thread's stack
  ThreadSnapshot::ExtraMemory()      per-thread extras
  ProcessSnapshot::ExtraMemory()     process-wide extras (on Windows, the PEB
                                     and each module's ExtraMemoryRanges)
  ExceptionSnapshot::ExtraMemory()   on Linux and Windows, memory that the
                                     crashing thread's registers point at
  ModuleSnapshot::CustomMinidumpStreams()
                                     arbitrary process memory that any linked
                                     module can register through
                                     CrashpadInfo::AddUserDataMinidumpStream

Thread records, register contexts and module lists are still written. The
Sentry stack-trace stream (kMinidumpStreamTypeSentryStackTraces) is added
directly by MinidumpFileWriter under CRASHPAD_ENABLE_STACKTRACE=ON, not through
CustomMinidumpStreams, so backtraces are unaffected by any of this.

tools/check-minidump.py asserts the result: a dump built with this patch has
zero-length thread stacks and an empty memory list.

Applies to sentry-native's external/crashpad submodule; see tools/buildsentry.sh
and ci/x64-windows/sentry.bat.

